From 9abe5060eda03dfa5be423e1e58bb2c8ef6534c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomokazu Katayama <s231143s@st.go.tuat.ac.jp>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:02:08 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 0019] PCG/smoothSolver: skip unused convergence residuals

Issue #68, port of the 0005 idea onto Bonus 7e246ee5 (including general
one-reduction DIC-PCG and final-sweep GaussSeidel residuals). This is a
FIXED-ITERATION REFERENCE EXPERIMENT ONLY, shown separately and not counted
in Bonus. It relies on the benchmark's fixed iteration controls; it is not
an unconditional optimization for convergence-controlled solves.

Select smoothSolver only with tolerance == 0, relTol == 0 and positive
nSweeps. Select PCG only with minIter >= maxIter > 0, for either DIC or the
ordinary PCG branch. Do not broaden PCG to zero-tolerance solves with other
bounds, or smoothSolver to positive-tolerance min/max-forced solves. Keep
initial Amul, normalisation, initial residual and initial convergence check,
all solution updates, preconditioner calls, iteration increments and the
original loop predicates. Non-selected controls retain the current work.
Also retain the original work when log >= 2 or solverPerformance::debug >= 2:
checkConvergence then prints intermediate residuals, making them observable.

For smoothSolver, determine whether the upcoming nSweeps batch is the last
one using both iteration bounds. Nonfinal fixed batches skip residual
formation and gSumMag. The final batch uses exactly the current residual
path: the 0016 fused sweep and Bnew-Bold completion when eligible, otherwise
the existing matrix.residual/internalResidual plus interface updates.
Never substitute an explicit b-A*psi evaluation for 0016's fused final norm:
its reassociated rounding is already part of the current Bonus baseline.

For fused GS, use gaussSeidelSweep<N,false> when no active component needs
a residual. This removes the division-defect/delta work, incoming U-only
correction traversal, residual writes and boundary completion, as well as
the separate sumMag pass and global reduction. Keep every cached interface
exchange: B(psi_new) still seeds the next sweep. The final exchange is also
needed to complete the reported residual. Thus N batches of s sweeps still
use N*s+1 exchanges per component, excluding the unchanged initial Amul.
For unfused smoothers, omit the entire unused explicit residual evaluation,
including its interface exchange; leave the smoother's own work unchanged.

Keep the current N=3/N=1 sweep grouping and each component's active state.
If all three components still smooth and only some need a residual, retain
the N=3 residual kernel (including unused accumulation for the other
components), but complete/reduce only the residuals that are needed.
Splitting such a sweep into N=1 kernels caused a bit mismatch with GCC 13
and -ffp-contract=fast in the standalone test; retaining grouping avoids it.
The final residual field is freshly initialized by the same kernel and
completed in the same order, independently of stale intermediate fields.

For DIC-PCG, omit the unused local L1 additions from the startup and
nonfinal product loops. Keep the two dot-product statements, all CG
recurrences, and the FixedList<solveScalar,3> reduction type/count/op and
communicator. Supply zero in the unused L1 slot. Shrinking the collective
to two scalars could change MPI's algorithm/summation tree and hence CG
rounding, which cannot be ruled out for Cray MPICH here. No DIC collective
is removed: an N-step solve still uses N+1 packed reductions, plus setup.
The final (0,0,sumMag(rA)) reduction is identical to the current code.
For ordinary PCG, defer the standalone gSumMag to solve exit, removing N-1
norm passes and reductions for an N-step zero-tolerance fixed solve.

checkConvergence also writes solverPerformance.converged(). With positive
PCG tolerances retain the norm at its last scheduled check, maxIter-1,
although it cannot end the solve. Earlier checks may see a stale finalResidual
but cannot affect the fixed count, and their flags are overwritten before
return. On an early singularity break, lazily evaluate the last successful
update's residual and restore its convergence flag when that check was
scheduled. DIC uses the same three-scalar collective and sumMag cell order
for this recovery (one extra collective only on such a breakdown); ordinary
PCG uses gSumMag. A break before any update preserves the initial residual.
Initial/final residuals, nIterations, converged/singular flags, final residual
field hooks, setFinished and solver residual log lines thus retain their
baseline values. Timing and communication-debug traces naturally differ.

Expected bit-identical coefficient.dat and solver residual lines against
7e246ee5 under identical compiler/MPI settings, not against unpatched v2512.
No floating-point solution expression is reassociated and no precision or
compiler flags change. Keeping MPI payload size preserves the conditions
for the same reduction tree; actual cluster identity still needs validation.

Validation: GCC 13.3 -fsyntax-only on PCG.C, smoothSolver.C (instantiating
all used sweep kernels) and simpleFoam.C, using b2-COMMON/check-all flags.
Standalone tests with both -ffp-contract=off and fast at -O3 -march=native:
43,200 cases per mode execute baseline/patched production PCG scalarSolve
bodies with mock matrix/preconditioner/serial collectives and compare psi,
residual fields, CG products, every returned performance field and logs
bitwise. Forced breakdown, min/max, positive/zero tolerances and diagnostic
fallbacks are covered. 30,720 scalar/batched GS cases per mode reuse the
production sweep and fake two-rank interfaces, with 225,628 per-sweep bit
comparisons; final fields/norms, counts, flags, logs and work counts agree.
These are local standalone checks, not full OpenFOAM/MPI timing or Cd runs.
